WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
In Swift, you are trained to never trust an untyped value.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
You rely on static typing to catch mistakes and prevent runtime crashes.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.000
JavaScript strips all of that away, allowing any variable to hold any value at any time.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
TypeScript is the strict superset layer designed to restore order.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
It gives you back the compiler-enforced contract you already rely on to write save code,

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
but the architecture is different. Swift bakes its types directly into the compiled machine code.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
TypeScript typebaritations exist only during development.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Before the code ever runs in the browser or on the device, the types are completely erased.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Because of this erasure, all of that strict type checking costs exactly zero performance overhead at runtime.

10
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.000
The best mental model is to view TypeScript's supervision over JavaScript,

11
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
the same way you view Swift's type system operating over legacy crash-prone objective C patterns.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
It is an analytical layer sitting on top of a dynamic runtime.

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
TypeScript provides a safety model but mirrors Swift.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.000
However, maintaining that model requires you to configure specific boundaries before the code ever reaches the compiler.

15
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
By default, TypeScript is lenient to make migrating old JavaScript code bases easier.

16
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
This contradicts the entire Swift philosophy.

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
To fix this, you start by enabling the no implicit any flag.

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
This acts directly like Swift's compiler, establishing a ban on untyped variables.

19
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Next, you enable strict null checks.

20
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
This flag replicates Swift optional.

21
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
It prevents nulls from silently bypassing your type checks and causing crashes and production.

22
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
With the compiler locked down, the basic primitives map over cleanly.

23
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Swift's string, int, or double, and bool translate directly to TypeScript's string, number, and boolean.

24
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
When you genuinely don't know what a value is, TypeScript provides the unknown type.

25
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
This forces you to logically narrow the type before you use it, exactly as you would with an as-question mark cast in Swift.

26
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:12.000
You must avoid TypeScript's any keyword, using any disables the compiler for that variable, removing your safety net.

27
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Without enforcing strict mode and utilizing unknown for ambiguous data, the environment is compromised.

28
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
It cannot provide the compile time safety you expect.

29
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Swift's optional type, T-question mark, is split into two distinct values in TypeScript, null and undefined.

30
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
null represents an intentionally absent value, like a missing database field.

31
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Undefined means a value simply hasn't been set yet.

32
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:46.000
When strict mode is enabled, the compiler handles both of these knowledge states by forcing you to explicitly unwrap them,

33
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
mirroring how Swift forces you to handle nil.

34
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Moving to structures, Swift relies heavily on struct and class.

35
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.000
In TypeScript, you map these to interface and type aliases.

36
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.000
But there is a catch. TypeScript has no concept of a value type struct.

37
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
At runtime, every complex shape you build operates as a reference type.

38
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
So while your structural type definitions will look similar to Swift's structs on the surface,

39
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.000
you have to remember that underneath the syntax, JavaScript is still executing reference-based behavior.

40
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Swift enums are first-class types.

41
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.000
TypeScript does have a built-in enum keyword, but it generates non-standard runtime code that causes unexpected behavior.

42
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Instead, map simple Swift enums to TypeScript string unions.

43
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
This represents the state as a series of specific, acceptable, literal strings.

44
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
To replicate the stability of Swift's raw value, you use a plain object mapped with the as-const assertion.

45
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
This creates a stable read-only reference.

46
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.000
But for complex structures, we need a different approach.

47
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
When translating Swift enums carrying associated values, use TypeScript discriminated unions.

48
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Create a shared literal field, often called status.

49
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
This field acts as the discriminant, serving as the pivot point for the union.

50
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.000
When checking this discriminant field, the compiler automatically narrows the type to that specific branch.

51
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.000
This narrowing provides the same exhaustiveness guarantees you get using a Swift statement.

52
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Mastering discriminated unions allows you to model complex, mutually exclusive states using the same logic you would apply to an enum heavy architecture in Swift.

53
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.000
Because TypeScript erases all types at compile time, it has a vulnerability.

54
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.000
When a JSON payload arrives over the network, TypeScript cannot verify its shape.

55
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.000
To the runtime, it is just an unknown object.

56
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
In Swift, you use codeable to ensure that JSON decoding matches your expected static types.

57
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
To bridge this runtime validation gap in JavaScript, you rely on a third-party library called ZOD.

58
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.000
With ZOD, you define your schema first.

59
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.000
This schema becomes the source of truth for the data shape you expect from the server.

60
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
ZOD parses the incoming payload at runtime.

61
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.000
If the payload is missing a required field or has the wrong data type, ZOD throws an error before that data can reach your app state.

62
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.000
From that exact same schema, ZOD automatically derives static TypeScript types.

63
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
You never have to write the type twice.

64
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.000
You define the validation once and you get both runtime checking and compile-time static types.

65
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.000
It is a close replication of the codeable experience.

66
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Relying on TypeScript interfaces alone for API calls creates a gap in your TypeSafety.

67
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.000
ZOD bridges that gap by validating data before it flows into the rest of your application.

68
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:45.000
There is one final utility to align TypeScript with Swift's precision, the Satisfies operator.

69
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.000
It validates object shapes without losing inferred literal types.

70
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:57.000
When applying generic typing to a TypeScript object, the compiler often widens specific literal values into broader base types.

71
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.000
But Satisfies checks the overall shape against an interface, preserving the narrowest possible inference inside.

72
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.000
In React Native, this operator is specifically useful for validating style sheet objects.

73
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.000
You get compiler validation for your style keys, while maintaining precise autocomplete for the values.

74
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Developing in React Native allows you to carry over Swift's rigorous logic by translating those familiar safety patterns into TypeScript syntax.

75
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:30.000
By enforcing strict null checks, isolating states with discriminated unions, and using ZOD for network boundaries,

76
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.000
you gain the same functional guard rails provided by the Swift compiler, catching errors long before they reach the user.

